WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.042
Film Courage: Let’s suppose you finish…you
have this thing where you want to always finish

00:00:03.042 --> 00:00:07.660
something [a screenplay]…but let’s suppose
you finish it and then you go back to it and

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:08.660
you’re like I’m not really that into it.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:09.660
Do you just leave it alone?

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.460
But the fact that you finished it was mainly
what was important to you?

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.990
Todd Berger, author/co-writer of The HappyTime
Murders: I’ll always want to get it to a

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:18.550
point where I can show it to other people.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:22.350
I’ve never done a first draft of anything
like “Meh?

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:26.540
I’m glad I wrote this but I’m never going
to show it to anyone.”

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:34.200
Because I feel you never know when you’re
going to meet a person who wants to do the

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.980
exact thing you had written five years ago?

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.170
So I always want to get a script to the place
where I would feel comfortable showing it

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:46.780
to someone else if it ever came up.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:50.271
I don’t show it to people, I would read
it, I would be happy with it, I would show

00:00:50.271 --> 00:00:55.830
it to my wife just to make sure it makes sense
and probably look for typos.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.410
And then I’m happy to put it on a shelf
and never show it to anybody.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:05.610
Unless one day I’m in a meeting, one day
I’m at a barbecue and someone’s like “Yeah,

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.170
I’m a producer.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:14.820
My brother owns a furniture store in Arizona
and I’d love…I can get you a free location

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:19.010
and I have half a million dollars and if I
can find a movie set in a furniture store

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:23.290
if I can shoot there and that’s when I’m
able to say…”Funny?

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:27.460
I have a thriller that I wrote set in a furniture
store five years ago.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:28.510
No one has ever seen it.

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:29.659
No one has ever read.

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:33.180
I just wrote it because I wanted to write
it, it was a story I wanted to tell but I

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.880
was very aware that there was no market for
a half million dollars furniture story movies

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.820
but then who knows?

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:44.600
The HappyTime Murders, D. Robertson and I
came up with these characters, came up with

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:45.600
the story, I wrote the script.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.200
We tried to get it made for fun, we just wanted
to for fun and it didn’t happen.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:55.150
Put it on the shelf, years later (we weren’t
doing anything with it), it was sitting on

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.870
a shelf (the virtual shelf of my computer).

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:03.310
My agent calls me, he says “Hey, the Jim
Henson company called me because they are

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:09.001
starting a new division called Henson Alternative
and they are looking to do grown up puppet

00:02:09.001 --> 00:02:15.751
stuff and they called and they’re like We
want to develop an R-rated puppet movie, do

00:02:15.751 --> 00:02:19.889
you have any writers who you think would be
a good fit to do that?”

00:02:19.889 --> 00:02:24.370
And my agent said “Well actually I have
a client who already wrote an R-rated puppet

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:28.189
movie, it’s called The HappyTime Murders,
he wrote it like five years ago.

00:02:28.189 --> 00:02:30.730
And they were like “What?”

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.330
So he sent it over and they read it and they
were like “Oh, yeah great, let’s do this.

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.209
That’s exactly what we wanted.”

00:02:36.209 --> 00:02:38.529
But we had already done it just for fun.

00:02:38.529 --> 00:02:44.609
So that’s always been my method it’s just
I write stuff for fun that I like and then

00:02:44.609 --> 00:02:48.700
I put it on the shelf and maybe one day it
will come up in conversation and someone will

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:49.890
want to make it.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:50.890
Who knows?

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:51.890
Film Courage: What makes it an R-rating?

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:59.420
What are these puppets doing that’s so grown
up?

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:06.780
Todd: The puppets are doing everything that
people do in their everyday life but we’re

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:11.209
finally seeing what the puppets do behind
closed doors.

00:03:11.209 --> 00:03:13.519
What the puppets do on the streets.

00:03:13.519 --> 00:03:19.969
How the puppets actually talk to each other.

00:03:19.969 --> 00:03:25.760
I’ve written a bunch of family stuff, I’ve
written a bunch of PG movies.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.019
But the movies I’ve directed have always
been R and they’re always filled with cursing

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:35.560
and grown up themes and people have asked
me “Why do you have these characters cursing

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:36.560
all the time?”

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.200
And I’m like “That’s how people talk.”

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.970
My friends (when we’re hanging out), that’s
how we talk.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:45.329
And when you want to make a movie about a
certain kind of person, they curse a lot.

00:03:45.329 --> 00:03:51.109
They drop swear words or this is how they
talk to each other and when we set out to

00:03:51.109 --> 00:03:57.430
make a movie about the actual…what if there
was an actual world where puppets and humans

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:02.239
coexisted with each other and the puppets
were like this underclass of society that

00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:11.739
were like these sugar addicts, living on the
streets and were this underclass, it would

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:15.049
be pretty rough and they would talk to each
other in pretty rough ways.

00:04:15.049 --> 00:04:25.480
It would be a really interesting world to
explore and the funny thing about R-rated

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:36.770
comedies is that a lot of movies you see there
is this very fine line between PG-13 and R,

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:37.770
right?

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.569
And so it’s very easy to take a script and
be like “Why don’t we just make this PG-13?”

00:04:40.569 --> 00:04:44.550
All you have to do is cut some swears out
of it.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:49.370
But if you are going to commit to creating
an R-rated movie, a lot of times studios or

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:54.199
production companies will say “If this is
going to be R, let make it R. Let’s go for

00:04:54.199 --> 00:04:56.830
it, you know?”

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:57.830
Because why not.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:03.050
We’ve already committed to appalling to
a certain kind of audience that wants an R-rated

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:08.680
comedy and we’ve alienated anyone under
the age of 16 who can’t go see it unless

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:10.460
they are with a parent or guardian.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:11.699
So we might as well go for it.

00:05:11.699 --> 00:05:19.360
There is no point in making an R-rated comedy
that are going to have one or two swear words

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.520
and that’s why it’s R. Why?

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.780
Let’s just go for it and get nuts, lets
show the sordid private lives of puppets and

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:28.240
all the shenanigans they get up to.

